@*
    The web addresses in a task title, as real links beside it: one anchor per
    distinct URL, in title order, each opening a new tab in a browser and the system
    browser from the desktop head, the way every external link in the product does.

    Beside the title and never inside it, because the title is the control that
    opens the task. The address in the title is drawn link-styled by TaskTitleText
    and a pointer can follow it there; this is the followable link a keyboard and a
    screen reader reach, so it sits where the host's other facts-to-follow sit — the
    row's and the card's badge slot, the panel's heading line — and that slot stops
    the press and the click, so following it is not opening the task.

    A linked badge, the shape the source badge beside it already has, labelled with
    the host and an arrow: on a dense row the host is the shortest label that still
    tells two links apart, and the arrow says it leaves the app. The accessible name
    is the whole address, "Open https://…", because a host alone does not say which
    page of it.

    Nothing at all for a title with no address, so a host can place it
    unconditionally.
*@
@foreach (var url in TitleRuns.Urls(Title))
{
    <Badge Kind="linked"
           Href="@url"
           Title="@url"
           CssClass="task-title-link"
           TestId="@TestId"
           target="_blank"
           rel="noopener noreferrer"
           aria-label="@($"Open {url}")">
        @TitleRuns.Host(url)<span class="task-title-link__arrow" aria-hidden="true"> ↗</span>
    </Badge>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The title, exactly as stored.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string? Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Carried by every anchor; a title with two addresses has two.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }
}
